name: reachability
boss: Portbreaker
flavor: I count the doors before you knock.
brief: Four questions, in order, when something will not connect: is the link
brief: up, does it have an address, is there a route, does dns work. Answer
brief: them in that order and you will almost never guess.
brief:
brief: ping proves a host answers. traceroute shows where the packets stop.
brief: netstat shows what your own machine is doing about it.
---
q: {does @ip@ even answer -- send three pings and stop|ping @ip@ three times, then stop|send exactly three pings to @ip@}, with ping
a: ping -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -c
hint: -c sets how many pings to send before stopping
---
setup: every ping here sends three, then stops
q: {just need loss percent -- ping @ip@ quietly, summary only|ping @ip@ with per-packet output suppressed, summary only|ping @ip@ and print only the final summary}
a: ping -c 3 -q @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -q
hint: -q suppresses per-packet lines, printing only the final summary
---
setup: every ping here sends three, then stops
q: {don't flood the link -- ping @ip@ one second apart|ping @ip@, spacing packets one second apart|space out the pings to @ip@ by one second each}
a: ping -i 1 -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -i
hint: -i sets the interval between packets, in seconds
---
setup: every ping here sends three, then stops
q: {two interfaces, test one specifically -- ping @ip@ out of @iface@|ping @ip@ through @iface@|send the pings to @ip@ out of @iface@}
a: ping -I @iface@ -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -I
hint: -I picks which interface the pings go out of
---
setup: every ping here sends three, then stops
q: {probe for MTU trouble -- ping @ip@ with a 1000 byte payload|ping @ip@ with a 1000 byte payload, to probe for MTU trouble|send 1000 byte pings to @ip@ to test for MTU issues}
a: ping -s 1000 -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -s
hint: -s sets the payload size in bytes, useful for MTU testing
---
q: {@ip@ may be dead -- give the ping run up after 5 seconds|cap the whole ping run to @ip@ at 5 seconds|stop pinging @ip@ after 5 seconds, whatever happens}, with ping
a: ping -w 5 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -w
hint: -w caps the whole run's wall-clock time, in seconds
---
q: pings aren't getting through -- %show% the path to @ip@
a: traceroute @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute
hint: traceroute with no flags shows the path packets take, hop by hop
---
q: {no dns wait per hop -- traceroute to @ip@ without resolving names|traceroute to @ip@ skipping name resolution for speed|run traceroute to @ip@ fast, with no hostname lookups}
a: traceroute -n @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -n
hint: -n skips resolving each hop's hostname, so it runs faster
---
q: {the traceroute to @ip@ is dragging on -- cap it at 10 hops|cap the traceroute to @ip@ at 10 hops|traceroute to @ip@ but stop after 10 hops}
a: traceroute -m 10 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -m
hint: -m caps the maximum number of hops traced
---
q: {a firewall may drop udp -- traceroute @ip@ with icmp echo|traceroute to @ip@ with icmp echo probes instead of udp|switch the traceroute to @ip@ over to icmp echo}
a: traceroute -I @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -I
hint: -I switches the probe type to icmp echo instead of udp
---
q: {traceroute to @ip@ out of @iface@|traceroute to @ip@ through @iface@|send the traceroute to @ip@ out of @iface@}
a: traceroute -i @iface@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -i
hint: -i picks which interface the probes go out of
---
q: {traceroute to @ip@ using @ip2@ as a chosen source address|traceroute to @ip@ with @ip2@ as the source you pick|set @ip2@ as the source address for a traceroute to @ip@}
a: traceroute -s @ip2@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -s
hint: -s sets the source address the probes claim to be from
---
q: {traceroute to @ip@, waiting at most 2 seconds for each probe|cap each probe's wait to 2 seconds on a traceroute to @ip@|traceroute to @ip@, waiting no more than 2 seconds per probe}
a: traceroute -w 2 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -w
hint: -w caps how long to wait for each probe's reply
---
q: {site loads by ip but not by name -- look up @host@'s address|resolve @host@ to its address|find the address @host@ resolves to}
a: nslookup @host@
why: live-net
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
hint: nslookup with just a name asks your configured resolver
---
q: {resolver's broken -- look up @host@ via @ip@ as dns server|resolve @host@ using @ip@ as the dns server you name|query @ip@ directly for @host@}
a: nslookup @host@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
hint: naming a server after the host queries it directly, bypassing your resolver
---
q: {see what your machine is doing on the network -- what sockets is it using|report the sockets this machine is currently using|list this machine's current sockets}
a: netstat
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat
hint: netstat alone lists the sockets currently in use
---
q: what's accepting connections -- %show% listening sockets, with netstat
a: netstat -l
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -l
hint: -l filters down to sockets in the listening state
---
q: %show% every socket, listening or not, with netstat
a: netstat -a
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -a
hint: -a shows every socket, listening and connected alike
---
q: dns is down -- %show% netstat sockets numerically, no names resolved
a: netstat -n
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -n
hint: -n skips resolving hostnames and service names to numbers
---
q: %show% only tcp sockets, with netstat
a: netstat -t
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
hint: -t filters to tcp sockets only
---
q: %show% only udp sockets, with netstat
a: netstat -u
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -u
hint: -u filters to udp sockets only
---
q: which process has a port open -- %show% who owns it, with netstat
a: netstat -p
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -p
hint: -p adds the pid and process name that owns each socket
---
q: %show% every listening tcp socket numerically, with its netstat process
a: netstat -tlnp
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
hint: single-letter flags combine -- tlnp is t, l, n and p together
---
q: %show% the routing table using netstat
a: netstat -r
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -r
hint: -r switches netstat to print the routing table instead of sockets
---
q: %show% unix domain sockets, with netstat
a: netstat -x
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -x
hint: -x filters to unix domain sockets
---
q: %show% raw sockets, with netstat
a: netstat -w
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -w
hint: -w filters to raw sockets
---
q: %show% netstat output with extended information
a: netstat -e
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -e
hint: -e adds extended fields like user and inode
---
q: ipv6 addresses keep getting cut off -- %show% netstat with wide columns
a: netstat -W
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -W
hint: -W widens columns so long addresses print in full
---
q: {ping6 the ipv6 loopback ::1 three times|send three ping6 probes to the ipv6 loopback ::1|ping6 ::1, the ipv6 loopback, stopping after three}
a: ping6 -c 3 ::1
why: live-net
ref: ping6
teach: ping6
hint: the whole world is moving to ipv6; the tools have a 6 on the end
---
q: {trace the ipv6 path to ::1|trace the path to the ipv6 loopback ::1|show the ipv6 path a trace to ::1 takes}
a: traceroute6 ::1
why: live-net
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6
hint: traceroute6 is traceroute's ipv6-only counterpart
---
q: {traceroute6 the ipv6 path to ::1 without resolving names|traceroute6 to ::1, skipping name resolution|traceroute6 the ipv6 path to ::1 with no hostname lookups}
a: traceroute6 -n ::1
why: live-net
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -n
hint: -n skips name resolution here too
---
q: {traceroute6 the ipv6 path to ::1, no further than 10 hops|cap the traceroute6 to ::1 at 10 hops|traceroute6 over ipv6 to ::1, stopping after 10 hops}
a: traceroute6 -m 10 ::1
why: live-net
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -m
hint: -m caps hops the same way it does for traceroute
